Apple is "pretty much done" with M6 chips, <em>Bloomberg</em>'s <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/mark-gurman/">Mark Gurman</a> reiterated today <a href="https://youtu.be/DZkRGgQn6CA">on his podcast</a>. Apple debuted the M6 chip in the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/mac-mini/">Mac mini</a> and has plans for an M6 <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/imac/">iMac</a> and entry-level 14-inch <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a>, but the company is not planning to make M6 Pro, M6 Max, or M6 Ultra chips.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/04/MacBook-Pro-Low-Angle-Wide-Lens.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1046019" /><br/>
Apple will instead go from the M5 Pro, M5 Max, and M5 Ultra to the M7 lineup. The upcoming <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">OLED</a> &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; models will use the same M5 Pro and M5 Max chips Apple uses in the current &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; models, with no chip upgrade planned until 2027. The M5 Pro and M5 Max came out in March.<br/>
<br/>
Gurman says the high-end touchscreen &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; models will get M7 Pro and M7 Max chips "at the end of next year." A refreshed low-end &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; with an M7 chip will launch next year, and an M7 Ultra chip is slated for the first half of 2028. It's unlikely we'll get a new <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/mac-studio/">Mac Studio</a> in 2027 with the next Ultra chip planned for 2028.<br/>
<br/>
